List<String> trades = List.of("TRD-001", "TRD-002", "TRD-003", "TRD-004", "TRD-005");

// SEQUENTIAL: both return TRD-001
Optional<String> first = trades.stream().findFirst();     // TRD-001 (always)
Optional<String> any   = trades.stream().findAny();       // TRD-001 (always — sequential)

// PARALLEL: findFirst still returns TRD-001, findAny returns whichever thread finds first
Optional<String> firstP = trades.parallelStream().findFirst();  // TRD-001 (deterministic)
Optional<String> anyP   = trades.parallelStream().findAny();    // TRD-003? TRD-001? (varies!)

// With filter — findFirst always returns first match in source order:
Optional<String> firstMatch = trades.parallelStream()
    .filter(t -> t.endsWith("3") || t.endsWith("5"))
    .findFirst();  // Always TRD-003 (first in source order that matches)

Optional<String> anyMatch = trades.parallelStream()
    .filter(t -> t.endsWith("3") || t.endsWith("5"))
    .findAny();    // TRD-003 or TRD-005 (whichever thread hits first)
```

**Why findFirst is slower in parallel:**

```java
// findFirst in parallel:
// Source: [1-250] → Thread1, [251-500] → Thread2, [501-750] → Thread3, [751-1000] → Thread4
// Filter: looking for n > 600
// Thread3 finds 601 first — BUT must check with Thread1 and Thread2
//   "Did you find anything < 601?"
//   Thread1: "No matches in [1-250]" — Thread2: "No matches in [251-500]"
//   Thread3: "OK, 601 is indeed the FIRST match"
// This coordination = overhead!

// findAny in parallel:
// Thread3 finds 601 → "FOUND ONE!" → returns immediately
// No coordination with other threads!
```

